To capture user input from a login form using JavaScript, you can use the following approach. This example assumes you have a simple HTML form with fields for a username and password. The JavaScript will capture the input when the form is submitted.

Here's a basic example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Login Form</title>
</head>
<body>
    <form id="loginForm">
        <label for="username">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" id="username" name="username" required>
        <br>
        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="password" name="password" required>
        <br>
        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
            event.preventDefault(); // Prevent the form from submitting the traditional way

            // Capture the input values
            const username = document.getElementById('username').value;
            const password = document.getElementById('password').value;

            // Log the captured values (for demonstration purposes)
            console.log('Username:', username);
            console.log('Password:', password);

